.TH "protocol_detect_version" "3" "2026\-02\-01" "bash\-server 0.1.0" "bash\-server library"
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IP
.EX
#include \(dqserver.h\(dq

int protocol_detect_version(int fd, char *first_byte);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
Detects the protocol version of an incoming client connection by peeking
at the first byte using \f[CR]recv(2)\f[R] with \f[CR]MSG_PEEK\f[R].
The byte is stored in \f[CR]*first_byte\f[R] but is not consumed from
the socket buffer, allowing the subsequent protocol handler to read it
normally.
.PP
The detection logic is a three\-way classification:
.PP
.TS
tab(@);
lw(23.7n) lw(23.7n) lw(22.7n).
T{
First byte value
T}@T{
Detected protocol
T}@T{
Return value
T}
_
T{
0x00 \(en 0x05 (channel ID)
T}@T{
Binary v2 framing
T}@T{
\f[CR]PROTOCOL_V2\f[R] (2)
T}
T{
0x7B (\f[CR]{\f[R])
T}@T{
NDJSON v2
T}@T{
\f[CR]PROTOCOL_V2_NDJSON\f[R] (3)
T}
T{
>= 0x20 (other printable)
T}@T{
Text v1 protocol
T}@T{
\f[CR]PROTOCOL_V1\f[R] (1)
T}
.TE
.PP
This classification is unambiguous because: \- No v1 command starts with
\f[CR]{\f[R].
\- No v1 command starts with a byte in the 0x00\(en0x05 range.
\- v2 binary frames always begin with a channel byte (0\(en5).
\- NDJSON lines always begin with \f[CR]{\f[R].
